      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What's the difference between everyday worry and anxiety worth getting help for?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Worry tends to come and go with the situation that caused it. Anxiety becomes worth attention when it stops tracking the circumstances: the loop keeps running after the problem is handled, it shows up in your sleep, your body, or your concentration, and you find yourself organizing your life around avoiding it. That shift, from a passing reaction to a pattern that runs on its own, is usually the point where therapy is useful."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How does therapy actually help with anxiety?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "We start by identifying what sets the loop off and what keeps it going, because anxiety is usually maintained by the very things people do to manage it. From there the work is practical: interrupting the worry cycle, learning to settle the physical response, and changing your relationship to the thoughts rather than arguing with them. The methods are evidence-informed, drawing on CBT and ACT, and the pace is set by what you're actually dealing with."
