    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section class="section" id="faq">
      <div class="container">
        <span class="eyebrow">Common Questions</span>
        <h2>Questions people ask before starting anxiety therapy.</h2>
        <p class="section-lead">A few of the things people most often ask before reaching out.</p>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <div class="faq-item reveal">
            <h3>Do you offer anxiety therapy in Houston (Clear Lake), TX?</h3>
            <p>Yes. I'm David Robles, LMSW, and I work with adults on anxiety and overthinking in the Clear Lake and greater Houston area, with in-person sessions nearby and secure telehealth available anywhere in Texas.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item reveal">
            <h3>Do you work with clients in Pasadena, TX?</h3>
            <p>Yes, by telehealth. Pasadena is a short drive from the Clear Lake office, and many people there prefer video sessions rather than adding a commute to an already full week. Sessions are secure and available anywhere in Texas, so where you live in the state doesn't limit whether we can work together.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item reveal">
            <h3>What's the difference between everyday worry and anxiety worth getting help for?</h3>
            <p>Worry tends to come and go with the situation that caused it. Anxiety becomes worth attention when it stops tracking the circumstances: the loop keeps running after the problem is handled, it shows up in your sleep, your body, or your concentration, and you find yourself organizing your life around avoiding it. That shift, from a passing reaction to a pattern that runs on its own, is usually the point where therapy is useful.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item reveal">
            <h3>How does therapy actually help with anxiety?</h3>
            <p>We start by identifying what sets the loop off and what keeps it going, because anxiety is usually maintained by the very things people do to manage it. From there the work is practical: interrupting the worry cycle, learning to settle the physical response, and changing your relationship to the thoughts rather than arguing with them. The methods are evidence-informed, drawing on CBT and ACT, and the pace is set by what you're actually dealing with.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item reveal">
            <h3>How long does anxiety therapy usually take?</h3>
            <p>It depends on what's driving it and how long the pattern has been in place. Some people come with a specific, recent problem and need a focused stretch of work. Others are unwinding something that's been running for years. I'll be straightforward with you about what I'm seeing rather than keeping you in sessions indefinitely, and we'll revisit where things stand as we go.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item reveal">
            <h3>Are you accepting new clients for anxiety therapy?</h3>
            <p>Yes, I'm currently accepting new clients. The first step is a free 15-minute consultation, a low-pressure way to talk through what's going on and see whether it's a good fit before committing to anything.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item reveal">
            <h3>How much does anxiety therapy cost, and do you take insurance?</h3>
            <p>Out-of-pocket sessions are $150, confirmed up front with no surprises, and sliding-scale spots ($80&ndash;$125) are available when cost is a barrier. Many major plans may be covered through partner networks, including Blue Cross Blue Shield of Texas, UnitedHealthcare, Aetna, Cigna, Humana, Ambetter (Superior HealthPlan), Molina Healthcare, Community Health Choice, Medicaid and CHIP, and Medicare. Reach out and we'll verify your benefits before the first session.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item reveal">
            <h3>I've been dealing with anxiety for years and I think I finally need to talk to someone. How do I find the right therapist in Houston?</h3>
            <p>Start by looking for someone who specializes in anxiety specifically, not a generalist who lists everything. You want a therapist who can explain what's actually driving it, not just hand you a breathing exercise. That's the work I do. I'm David Robles, a licensed therapist in the Houston (Clear Lake), TX area working specifically with anxiety, overthinking, and the patterns that keep it going. I offer a free 15-minute consultation so you can get a feel for the fit before committing to anything. Sessions are available by telehealth across all of Texas, so you don't need to be local.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

